ive to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-7,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-7)+2=-4.\n$$" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416393 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416397 1069 "q-140374" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416397 1071 "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & b & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ x+z \\\\ y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416397 1072 #uuid "5979cff9-340a-5b78-84fd-5ca3a8c1f370" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416397 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-5,$ $b=3,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+3+2=0.\n$$" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416394 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416398 1069 "q-82461" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416398 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}.$ The matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & b & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416398 1072 #uuid "abf9f8e2-5bef-5f02-8f73-975c228317db" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416398 1074 "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $B:$\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=3,$ $c=3$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+3+3=8.\n$$" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416395 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416399 1069 "q-140360" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416399 1071 "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}6x-7y \\\\ -2x-6y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416399 1072 #uuid "e8f9b1b9-d1e3-5a9e-a4fd-34ae92b559e5" 13194139534259 17592186045417 true] [17592186416399 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}50 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 50 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & 